From d6085af5dfd6f1abaa465f129fb0fbf7bfe70a4f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Metacraft Labs <info@metacraft-labs.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 00:22:34 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Make reconcileStaleJobs able to see and reap scale set jobs
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

reconcileStaleJobs() exists so that a job GARM believes is queued, but
which the forge has since finished, does not sit in the database for
ever. On any installation that uses runner scale sets it has never been
able to do that, for two independent reasons.

First, it selects its candidates with ListEntityJobsByStatus(), which
hard-filters `workflow_job_id > 0`. Scale set jobs are recorded by
workers/scaleset from the runner-scale-set message protocol, which has
no workflow job ID at all — ScaleSetJobMessage.ToJob() populates
ScaleSetJobID and leaves WorkflowJobID zero. The candidate list is
therefore guaranteed to exclude every scale set job, and on a
scale-set-only controller reconcileStaleJobs() is a no-op: it lists
nothing, calls the forge for nothing and deletes nothing.

Second, the reconciler addresses jobs by WorkflowJobID throughout: the
"recently checked" map, the advisory lock key, the GitHub lookup and
store.DeleteJob(), which resolves rows with `WHERE workflow_job_id = ?`.
Because every scale set row carries workflow_job_id = 0, simply
relaxing the filter would collapse every scale set job of an entity
onto one map key and one lock key, ask GitHub for job 0, and then have
DeleteJob(ctx, 0) First()-match and delete an arbitrary scale set row —
quite possibly one that is running.

Fix both:

  * ListEntityJobsByStatus() now accepts a row that carries either
    forge-side identifier: `workflow_job_id > 0 OR scale_set_job_id
    <> ''`. Rows with neither remain excluded.

  * Add store.DeleteJobByID(), which resolves the row by its own
    primary key — the one identifier both job kinds always have — and
    use it from reconcileStaleJobs(). reconcileStaleJobs() now keys its
    dedupe map and its advisory lock on params.Job.ID as well.

  * Scale set jobs have no workflow job endpoint to query, so check
    them at the granularity that does exist: the workflow run. Add
    GithubClient.GetWorkflowRunByID() (satisfied for free by the
    embedded *github.ActionsService) and treat a scale set job as stale
    only when its run has status "completed", or when the run 404s.
    Anything short of completed — queued, in_progress, waiting,
    requested, pending — is left alone, so a genuinely queued job is
    never reaped.

Two callers relied on the old filter to keep scale set jobs away from
pool machinery, so guard them explicitly now that the filter is
relaxed: scaleDown()'s stale-job cleanup and consumeQueuedJobs() both
skip rows with WorkflowJobID == 0. Neither can serve a scale set job,
and both would otherwise call DeleteJob/LockJob/UnlockJob with a job ID
of 0. consumeQueuedJobs() could already reach that state today, because
the pool watcher adds scale set jobs to the in-memory job map without
consulting the filter at all.

Signed-off-by: Metacraft Labs <info@metacraft-labs.com>
